  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>  This web-log comes out of a few different motivations. My time at the University of St Andrews, where I graduated just over a year ago, let me engage with economics 
  and finance in two ways at once. Some of it was structured, through my studies, and some of it was independent, following whatever topics happened to catch my 
  interest. I've noticed that my career so far has tended towards the former. While applying the ideas I learned in university to real world problems has been fulfilling, 
  it leaves less room for the latter kind, where I get to follow my own curiosity. This project is my way of keeping that second strand going. After all, the process
  of learning is never really finished, and it is by continuing to learn about these topics that I will sustain my interest in them and continue growing in my career. </p>

  <p> A more immediate motivation has been the effect of AI on my capacity to think independently and originally. As these tools have grown more capable and 
  accessible, I have noticed myself getting intellectually lazier as I increasingly reach for them to replace my work instead of as a tool to make certain processes 
  or efficient, or my work more productive. Therefore, I aim to use AI in this project only for things like coding, and not for the writing, the research, or the 
  working-out of ideas.
  </p>

  <p> Following this, another motivating reason for this project is that it will provide a framework through which the skills needed for my career, such as investment
  analysis and economics, will be improved. Its scope will be as broad as my interests are, and will take in general macroeconomics and market commentary, analysis 
  of individual credits, tests of different models of credit analysis, evaluations of different investment strategies, and a closer look at the particular quirks 
  and opportunities of different corners of the credit market.</p>

  <p> Finally, I want to note the creative debts this web-log owes. On typography and the general style of the site, <a href="https://www.legalstyle.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"> Notes on the Style of Law</a> 
  has been my main inspiration. As I write more, I will credit the people I have found most useful here. </p>

